A||||5||Fleabitdog||Adding cruise  control||||1||
Z||000000||Fleabitdog||09-15-2000||03:21 PM||||I have an 88GT, It does not have cruise control. How difficult is it to add a cruise control? Do I need the whole column or can I just add the stalk? Which would be easier? If I need to add the whole column I am in need of a clean used one. Thanks Dog ||||reg||1
Z||000001||286SE'S||09-15-2000||04:03 PM||||I can only answer your stalk question. No, you do not need a new column. The CC stalk is the same as the non-CC. It just has a wire comming out of the bottom that goes into the column and runs down it (along the wiper/turn signal wires) to a connector it mates to under the dash. You will need to remove the steering wheel and lock plate to run this wire. <BR>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-15-2000||08:37 PM||||The consensus is to install an aftermarket cruise. These usually come with the new stalk for the steering column. You will likely need to take off the steering wheel and lockplate to install the stalk.<P>The rest is done acording to the instructions that come with the kit.||||reg||1
Z||000003||TOM||09-15-2000||10:02 PM||||I personally would install a factory cruise. But I have access to all the parts. I know it would be more work. But I like my stuff to look right ||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||09-16-2000||01:22 AM||||I don't see where the column needs to come apart. The cruise is just a new turn signal lever with wires running down the column. Use a stiff wire and weave it down the bottom of the column and pull the cruise wires through. The module goes on the left side of the console below the shifter in the indentation. The cruise servo and brake/clutch switches have mounts already in place. Routing the wires to the back will be fun but not impossible. The speedo feeds a signal to the module. You might luck out and have the wires tucked up in there, but knowing GM, noooooooo. That would cost another $2.00. Grab a manual. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||09-16-2000||01:26 AM||||I was playing with my old Ciera cruise module and pin B appears to be the cruise lamp output. If you connect a bulb from +12 and pin B, it should light when cruise is active.<P>The Fiero module is likely the same one. Someone should try it. No warranty expressed or implied. Knowing GM, they bought modules with the driver left off for the Fiero.||||reg||3
